13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {After reviewing the round, I’m shifting slightly from my earlier emphasis on E. While aburidashi is a documented ninja practice, there is no clear precedent in surviving Iga or Kōga manuscripts for its systematic use across an entire section of a compiled manual like the Bansenshukai, nor any indication that every copyist would have uniformly recorded the same blank circles rather than attempting to reproduce the underlying strokes.  

By contrast, the Oniwaban’s documented role in controlling and sanitizing sensitive intelligence materials during the Edo period offers a tighter institutional fit for why identical omissions appear in every lineage of the text. That mechanism aligns with how other military and espionage manuals were handled once they entered Shogunate custody.  

If we accept that level of coordinated redaction as plausible, then several other options (including the political-conspiracy framing in C) become harder to sustain on chronological grounds alone. This leaves H as still the clearest outlier—an entirely invented esoteric-sexual framework with no textual or archival support—but I now see D as carrying more explanatory weight than I initially allowed, which narrows the field of truly implausible choices.}

