13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 7’s timeline objection to D is decisive and worth hardening. The Oniwaban as an institution only coalesced decades after 1676, so any claim that it imposed uniform redactions on an already-circulating Iga/Toda-family manuscript is chronologically impossible; the Oniwaban’s documented remit was also limited to the shogun’s household, giving it neither mandate nor reach to police every provincial copy. That leaves the identical blank pattern far more consistent with an archetype-level omission (authorial, moral, or encoding) than with later centralized intervention by a body that did not yet exist.  

By contrast, the political-redaction scenario in C, while still speculative, at least operates inside the correct century and among attested actors who could plausibly have influenced early copies. The same holds for the moral self-censorship, physical-wear, or aburidashi hypotheses. H remains the clearest outlier because it alone requires an entirely unattested esoteric-sexual superstructure with no anchor in any surviving shinobi text or transmission practice.  

If the goal is to identify the single least historically grounded explanation, D’s anachronistic institutional actor makes it the stronger candidate for elimination over the other remaining options.}

